    "case_id": "301",
    "case_summary": "A patient in their mid-80s presented to the emergency department with complaints of intermittent confusion and falls. The patient had a medical history of moderate aortic stenosis and renal cell carcinoma treated with radical nephrectomy followed by chemotherapy and radiation.\n\nThe patient was alert, fully oriented, and in no acute distress, with a heart rate of 89 beats/min and blood pressure of 127/58 mm Hg. Frequent pauses were noted on telemetry.\n\n- ECG findings:    Image Title: 12-Lead Electrocardiogram and Intracardiac Recordings    Image Description: Shows 6 P waves conducting to the ventricle, followed by a period of atrioventricular block. Block initiates after a premature atrial complex and transiently resolves following another premature atrial complex.      - Intracardiac recordings:    Description: Recordings from catheters positioned in the right atrium and along the His bundle demonstrate conduction from the atrium through the His-Purkinje system to the ventricle during the first and second beats. Prolongation of the His-His interval from the second to third beats results in phase IV block within the His-Purkinje system.      - Echocardiogram:    Description: Transthoracic echocardiogram showed preserved biventricular systolic function and moderate aortic stenosis.",
